." rm(1)
.TH RM 1 "2026-04-23" "Substrate" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
rm \- remove files or directories
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B rm
.RI [ OPTION ]... " FILE" ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B rm
removes each named
.IR FILE .
Regular files, symbolic links, sockets, FIFOs, and device nodes are removed with
.BR unlinkat (2) .
Directories are removed only when
.BR -d
or
.BR -r
is supplied.
.PP
Recursive removal uses descriptor-relative traversal.
Directories are opened with
.BR openat (2) ,
inspected with
.BR fstatat (2) ,
enumerated through
.BR fdopendir (3) / readdir (3) ,
and removed with
.BR unlinkat (2)
using
.BR AT_REMOVEDIR .
Symbolic links are never followed during recursive traversal.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR -d , " --dir"
Remove empty directories.
.TP
.BR -f , " --force"
Ignore nonexistent operands, suppress ordinary error messages, and never prompt.
.TP
.B -i
Prompt before every removal.
.TP
.B -I
Prompt once before removing more than three operands or before any recursive removal.
.TP
.BI "--interactive" [ =WHEN ]
Prompt according to
.IR WHEN :
.BR never ,
.BR once ,
or
.BR always .
With no explicit value,
.B always
is used.
.TP
.BR -r , " -R , --recursive"
Remove directories and their contents recursively.
.TP
.B --one-file-system
Do not descend across filesystem boundaries during recursive removal.
Mount roots outside the starting filesystem are skipped with a warning.
.TP
.B --preserve-root
Refuse recursive removal of
.BR / .
This is the default.
.TP
.B --no-preserve-root
Disable the default recursive safeguard for
.BR / .
Use with extreme care.
.TP
.BR -v , " --verbose"
Print a line for each file or directory that is removed.
.TP
.B --help
Display usage information and exit.
.TP
.B --version
Display version information and exit.
.SH SAFETY
.B rm
refuses to remove operands whose final path component is
.B .
or
.BR .. .
.PP
With recursive removal and the default
.BR --preserve-root
policy,
.B rm
rejects
.B /
with the diagnostic:
.PP
.RS
it is dangerous to operate recursively on '/'
.RE
.PP
The command also warns when a recursive target resolves to the caller's
.B HOME
directory.
.SH INTERACTIVE BEHAVIOR
Prompts are written to standard error.
If standard input is not a terminal,
prompt-driven removals are skipped.
.PP
Answers beginning with
.B y
or
.B Y
confirm removal.
Any other response declines it.
.PP
Write-protected targets and sticky-directory ownership conflicts trigger prompts of the form:
.PP
.RS
rm: remove write-protected file 'path'?
.RE
.PP
Ordinary interactive prompts use the form:
.PP
.RS
rm: remove directory 'path'?
.RE
.SH TOCTOU MITIGATION
Recursive directory traversal keeps the parent directory descriptor open while processing its children.
Each child is identified relative to that descriptor instead of reconstructing path strings for every step.
This narrows the race window for symlink swaps and concurrent directory modifications.
.PP
When descending into a subdirectory,
.B rm
opens it with
.B O_NOFOLLOW
so a directory entry that becomes a symbolic link is rejected instead of being followed.
.SH PERMISSIONS AND IMMUTABLE FAILURES
Removal still depends on parent-directory permissions and filesystem policy.
If the filesystem rejects removal with errors such as
.BR EACCES ,
.BR EPERM ,
.BR EBUSY ,
.BR EROFS ,
or
.BR ENOTEMPTY ,
.B rm
reports the failure unless
.B -f
is active.
.PP
Immutable files are not cleared automatically.
If the filesystem returns
.B EPERM
for an immutable target,
.B rm
reports that failure and leaves the file unchanged.
.SH SIGNALS
.B rm
handles
.B SIGINT
and
.B SIGTERM
by stopping further work and exiting nonzero.
Already completed removals are not rolled back.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
0
All requested removals succeeded or were skipped by interactive decline.
.TP
1
At least one removal failed.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
rm file
Remove a single file.
.TP
rm -r dir
Remove a directory tree.
.TP
rm -d emptydir
Remove an empty directory.
.TP
rm -f missing
Ignore a missing operand.
.TP
rm -v file
Print a diagnostic while removing a file.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR rmdir (1),
.BR unlink (2),
.BR openat (2),
.BR fstatat (2),
.BR unlinkat (2),
.BR fdopendir (3)